{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310561,"nonce":"RjZT3W1J6EG4MKSZh0hANW8ZoLY=","title":"CI conformance runs are bimodal — either ~35 TIMEOUT/~200 CRASH or ~240 TIMEOUT/exactly 0 CRASH; not the CPU","message":"Consecutive CI runs alternate between two modes on the SAME commit range:\n\n    f67cc697  TIMEOUT=39   CRASH=199   AMD EPYC 9V74\n    a688c61f  TIMEOUT=239  CRASH=0     Intel Xeon 8573C\n    0388587e  TIMEOUT=238  CRASH=0     AMD EPYC 9V74\n    5fc004f8  TIMEOUT=34   CRASH=204   AMD EPYC 9V74\n    72c05da9  TIMEOUT=29   CRASH=209   Intel Xeon 8573C\n    bc05be8b  TIMEOUT=241  CRASH=0     Intel Xeon 8573C\n\nCRASH is EXACTLY 0 in the bad mode, so it is systematic, not noise: every test\nthat would crash instead runs to the 25s watchdog. That is ~200 x 25s of extra\nwall clock and it depresses the published score by several tests.\n\n**It is NOT the CPU** — both modes occur on both AMD and Intel. Ruled out by\nmeasurement, after the CPU correlation looked convincing on the first two runs.\n\nAlso ruled out: jemalloc. `ensure_jemalloc()` (src/main.rs, Linux-only) re-execs\nunder LD_PRELOAD when libjemalloc.so.2 exists, which varies by image and had the\nright shape — but the NUC HAS jemalloc and crashes cleanly either way\n(`RAYZOR_NO_JEMALLOC=1` changes nothing, exit 139 both times).\n\nREMAINING HYPOTHESIS, unproven: core dumps. GitHub images ship apport and\n`core_pattern` pipes to it; if the image leaves the core limit unlimited, each\ncrash streams a ~93 MB image through a collector on a 4-vCPU box running four\ntests at once. `haxe-conformance/run.sh` now sets `ulimit -c 0`, and the CI\nenvironment step records `core_pattern` and `ulimit -c` so the next runs say\nwhich mode they were in and whether cores were enabled. If a bad-mode run still\nappears with `core limit: 0`, this hypothesis is dead and the cause is elsewhere.\n\nCRASH COMPOSITION (local, 232 crashes): 104 uncompiled-function trap stubs,\n99 SIGSEGV, 11 SIGBUS, 9 stack overflow, 4 import failures, 3 SIGILL, 2 SIGKILL.\nThe two SIGKILLs are OOM — see [[bugs_enum_keyed_map_allocation_blowup]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_conformance_crash_timeout_bimodality_in_ci` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}